
#s3:use case
1: Media and Creative Assets
    • Images & Photos: JPEG, PNG, GIF, SVG,RAW files
        (e.g., website assets, user profile pictures,e-commerce product galleries).
    • Videos: MP4, MOV, AVI files (e.g., video-on-demand streaming libraries,
        security footage, raw production clips).
    • Audio Files: MP3, WAV, AAC files (e.g., podcasts, voice recordings,
        music streaming catalogs).
2: Big Data, Analytics, and AI
    Data Lakes & Warehouses: Massive datasets stored in CSV, Parquet, JSON,
    or ORC formats for business intelligence.
3: Documents and General Files
    Office & Text Documents: PDFs, Word documents, spreadsheets,
        and plain text files.

#s3:size handle
• Maximum object size: 50 TB per individual object (increased from 5 TB).
• Maximum single PUT upload size: 5 GB
    (objects larger than 5 GB must use multipart upload).we can set min size like 10mb,
    after that it will break in chunks.

#Fault tolerance(S3):
1. Built-in Multi-AZ Fault Tolerance (Intra-Region):By default S3 files
                      are stored in 3 different availability zones in the same region.
2: Cross-Region Replication (CRR) for Regional Fault Tolerance.

#how s3 is expose to end users:
Amazon only exposes regions for S3 to end users.
Amazon does not expose availability zones for S3 to end users.Therefore it's not
    user-configurable.


#how to create multipart upload in s3:

1. CreateMultipartUpload (Initiation)
/createMultipartupload(String filename)->will get uploadId

• What it does: Notifies Amazon S3 that you are about to upload a new object in parts.
• What it returns: S3 returns a unique UploadId. You must include this specific ID
  in all subsequent part uploads and the final completion request.
.uploadId is used to create presigned url.
. After this we need to create as many presigned url as chunks.
/generatepresignedurl(filename,uploadId,partnumber(chunksnumer))
.This presigned url to passed to frontend to upload.

2. UploadPart (Execution)

• What it does: Uploads the individual chunks of your file.
• How it works: You call this API repeatedly (sequentially or in parallel) for
  each part of the file. With each call, you must pass the UploadId and a unique
  PartNumber (from 1 to 10,000).
• What it returns: S3 returns an ETag header for each successfully uploaded part(chunks).
  You must store these ETags and their corresponding part numbers in a list;
  you will need them for the final step.
  An ETag (Entity Tag)->
    ETag Value Format:Standard MD5 Hex:"5d41402abc4b2a76b9719d911017c592"
    Hex Hash + Dash + Part Count:"a7c362...c54-3"

    Core Use Cases for ETags in S3->
        1:Data Integrity Checks

3. CompleteMultipartUpload (Completion)
/completeMultipartUpload(filename,uploadId,part(which contains etag))
• What it does: Tells S3 you have finished uploading all pieces.
• How it works: You pass the UploadId along with the list of part numbers and
  their respective ETag values that you collected
  in Step 2. S3 then stitches the parts together into a single file, cohesive object

what is presigned URL:
An Amazon S3 presigned URL is a secure, temporary link that grants
time-limited access to specific private S3 objects without requiring
AWS credentials or changing bucket policies.



